hello everyone and welcome to a really  awesome game from 2003 between peter  heinen nelson and luke mcshane i'm  showing this game as uh you guys have  probably already saw the interview chat  based india made with peter heinen  nelson but also with vladimir podkin uh  the the chief trainer of yani palmishi  for the world chess championship match  2021 so i just wanted to uh in case you  haven't do check it out it will be the  first link and the second link in the  description below uh so the interview uh  by chessbase india by uh with uh peter  heinen nielsen and vladimir podkin uh if  you guys want to learn a bit more about  what happened during the world  championship what happened behind the  scenes it's a very extensive interview  uh and uh well i i do not want to repeat  anything that was said in there so i  just decided to  show you one of uh one of peter's games  as i thought this was a really fun one  uh and just invite you to check out the  interviews now it's interesting uh this  is uh peter's eighth world chess  championship title and  what's also very interesting is that not  only did he work for both anand and  magnus carlson  but during their first match in 2013 uh  when carlson first challenged cross uh  anand for the title uh he did not work  for for either of them so he only  started working with magnus in 2014. so  interestingly as soon as he stepped down  as anand's uh second uh anand lost the  title and uh well so far uh he's been in  every world championship match magnus  played and magnus uh you know retains  the title every time so uh  definitely uh something peter has up his  sleeve that is you know allowing these  champions to just to stay on top but  that being said do check it out it's a  really awesome interview i've watched it  in its entirety so i i do recommend it  and let's check out this game it's a  very short one but it's uh you know very  enjoyable one uh so peter with the white  pieces opens with d4 we have knight to  f6 he's facing a very strong english  grandmaster luke mcshane no doubt to all  of you i have heard of him uh we have c4  g6 and now knight to c3 we have d5 so a  normal groomfield defense c captures on  d5 we have knight captures uh and e4 now  chasing away the knight of course we  have a captain c3 captures captures and  now bishop to g7  is pretty much always played here but  mcshane goes for the uh sort of an early  deviation with c5 i don't know the exact  difference this makes because obviously  you have to be very strong to  uh to take advantage of this but uh  maybe there really isn't any maybe it's  just uh you know different move order  but i doubt it because in the database  bishop to g7 is just like 20 000 games  and um you know c5 maybe like a thousand  games but okay uh it doesn't really  matter because they transpose into the  the standard line we have bishop to c4  bishop to g7 now the bishop is fine  herod uh controlling this very long  diagonal knight to e2 now preparing the  castle also adding more support to the  center knight to c6 black also fights  for the center and now bishop to e3 so  this is kind of the theme of this  opening we're fighting for that center  and now you could just castle and  continue the game but black decides to  relieve some of the tension in the  center he plays c captures on d4 c  captures and now queen to a5 check now  what does he want with this uh check  well he's asking why do you want to  block with the queen do you want to play  king to f1 and play some sort of a  game without the the early castles or do  you want to move the bishop back which  kind of  forces you to to waste the tempo yeah  and also uh you will not be defending  your d4 square as properly as you are  now so okay bishop back to d2 attacking  the queen queen back to d8 now with some  pressure on the d4 square and now white  uh  either goes back but that's like saying  okay i'm very happy with the draw here  uh or he advances d5 like uh it happened  in this game now what do you do here  obviously you are offering the rook for  a knight but not really if black  captures the rook white of course will  not capture on c6 white will capture the  bishop here attack the black rook on h8  and now after you tend to the rook of  course we're going to capture the knight  on c6 and white is just winning so  knight the e5 has to be played now the  bishop here is attacked but it doesn't  matter just bishop c3 and now  of course this is uh  very good for white because if you  capture on c4 we're gonna capture on g7  and okay you don't lose any material for  example queen to a for check you can  block bishop the seven queen captures on  c4 we're gonna capture here but after  white castles uh white has excellent  central control the rook is in g7 the  king is not castling so this would be  pretty bad for for black  so instead we have just castles uh and  now comes the bishop back to b3 so we  don't have to worry about this tension  here queen to b6 uh with some tricky  lines possible as df two pawn might  become a little bit weak so there might  be some ideas of maybe knight to g4  attacking the bishop here and also  threatening the f2 pawn but it doesn't  really work uh as peter will show so he  plays f4 attacks the knight and now if  you try something like this yes it looks  very scary queen f2 bishop captures and  c3 are the threats but just bishop d4  and you don't really have all that much  once everything gets traded off captures  captures captures white  you know has this beautiful center the  pieces are nicely developed you can just  castle and the black can't really hope  to  get all that much out of the position  here so instead after f4 we have knight  back to d7 and now we have a trade  captures captures and rook to c1  putting the rook on this uh only open  file on the board for the moment uh and  uh well preparing some very nasty stuff  because uh  the dark square bishops have left the  board and black really has some dark  square weaknesses around his king so you  want the white queen to  somehow gain control of this diagonal  so rook to d8  and now comes rook to c4 we moved the  rook and now we prepare to bring the  queen over to this diagonal e5 now uh  and here just queen to a1 uh not  allowing the capture now the the pawn  cannot move uh and uh interestingly uh  luke mcshane had this position after  this game some some three months uh uh  against another player jonathan parker  in the european cup and there he played  rook to e8 but here in the game against  nilsson he played f6 he uh well not only  defends the e5 pawn but he's now also  preparing e captures on f4 because now  the pawn can capture but there is one  thing that makes this move a bit of a  problem uh not just uh you know they  often say that you know f6 isn't uh  the best move in most positions but uh  for this position it is true uh because  now we have queen to c3 and by playing  f6 you of course weaken the seventh rank  and now rook to c7 uh well it's not an  uh it's not a very pleasant move to have  to face so here e captures on f4 black  continues with his plan knight captures  on f4 and now knight to e5 attacking the  rook on c4  rook the c7 check of course this was our  plan all along and now king to h8 and  now this video will have two very nice  positive video moments so this is the  first one feel free to pause the video  and try to find the only winning move  for white here it's not an easy one to  spot so while i give you a couple of  seconds  so for those of you who are able to do  it congratulations on spotting this  because it's uh well you really have to  see deep into the position to you know  uh spot such a move and for those of you  who just want to enjoy the show the move  is rook to f1 that's that's the good  stuff as you see the rook is cutting off  the king from the seventh rank so it's  uh uh well for the moment the king  cannot help out with the defense of the  f6 pawn and the f6 pawn is 